Walkers and the id+Index combo are great, they only suffer from a single drawback, they're extremely tedious to write.
This led to the `SchemaWalker` use of generics to be oriented towards reducing the amount of code we have to write and it's only exacerbated in `engine-v2` for operations with heavy use of `Deref`, forwarding to an embedded SchemaWalker, and few walkers because I've refactored the underlying structs so many times.

All of this came at the cost of reduced readability, using "go to definition" anywhere in `schema.walk(id)` would not lead to the walker. Requesting the type from rust-analyzer would produce something like `SchemaWalker<'a, ID>`. This produced hard to read code for anyone but me such as:
```rust
self
    .schema_walker
    .walk(&self.schema_walker.as_ref()[*id])
    .serialize(serializer)
```

@obmarg  found an excellent solution for this, generating most of the walker code from a GraphQL schema in `registry-v2` several months ago. Since then I've been working here and there on applying on `engine-v2-schema`, especially the last few weeks. Within that effort, I've tried to improve the readability of this design as much as I could. I ended up with the following design:


### Code generation

Code is generated with `engine-v2-codegen`, this is the output for [EnumValue](https://github.com/grafbase/grafbase/blob/gb-7452-use-code-generation-in-engine-v2-schema-like-registry-v2/engine/crates/engine-v2/schema/src/generated/enum_value.rs):
```rust
//! ===================
//! !!! DO NOT EDIT !!!
//! ===================
//! Generated with: `cargo run -p engine-v2-codegen`
//! Source file: <engine-v2-codegen dir>/domain/schema.graphql
use crate::{
    generated::{TypeSystemDirective, TypeSystemDirectiveId},
    prelude::*,
    StringId,
};
use walker::{Iter, Walk};

/// Generated from:
///
/// ```custom,{.language-graphql}
/// type EnumValue @meta(module: "enum_value") @indexed(id_size: "u32", max_id: "MAX_ID") {
///   name: String!
///   description: String
///   directives: [TypeSystemDirective!]!
/// }
/// ```
#[derive(Debug, serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize)]
pub struct EnumValueRecord {
    pub name_id: StringId,
    pub description_id: Option<StringId>,
    pub directive_ids: Vec<TypeSystemDirectiveId>,
}

#[derive(PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Clone, Copy, Hash, serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize, id_derives::Id)]
#[max(MAX_ID)]
pub struct EnumValueId(std::num::NonZero<u32>);

#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
pub struct EnumValue<'a> {
    pub(crate) schema: &'a Schema,
    pub(crate) id: EnumValueId,
}

impl std::ops::Deref for EnumValue<'_> {
    type Target = EnumValueRecord;
    fn deref(&self) -> &Self::Target {
        self.as_ref()
    }
}

impl<'a> EnumValue<'a> {
    /// Prefer using Deref unless you need the 'a lifetime.
    #[allow(clippy::should_implement_trait)]
    pub fn as_ref(&self) -> &'a EnumValueRecord {
        &self.schema[self.id]
    }
    pub fn id(&self) -> EnumValueId {
        self.id
    }
    pub fn name(&self) -> &'a str {
        &self.schema[self.as_ref().name_id]
    }
    pub fn description(&self) -> Option<&'a str> {
        self.as_ref().description_id.map(|id| self.schema[id].as_ref())
    }
    pub fn directives(&self) -> impl Iter<Item = TypeSystemDirective<'a>> + 'a {
        self.as_ref().directive_ids.walk(self.schema)
    }
}

impl Walk<Schema> for EnumValueId {
    type Walker<'a> = EnumValue<'a>;
    fn walk<'a>(self, schema: &'a Schema) -> Self::Walker<'a>
    where
        Self: 'a,
    {
        EnumValue { schema, id: self }
    }
}

impl std::fmt::Debug for EnumValue<'_> {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {
        f.debug_struct("EnumValue")
            .field("name", &self.name())
            .field("description", &self.description())
            .field("directives", &self.directives())
            .finish()
    }
}
```

 The whole schema is defined by [engine-v2-codegen/domain/schema.graphql](https://github.com/grafbase/grafbase/blob/gb-7452-use-code-generation-in-engine-v2-schema-like-registry-v2/engine/crates/engine-v2/codegen/domain/schema.graphql) which generates the whole `generated` module from `engine-v2-schema`. Generated code usually includes the `XRecord` which is stored in the `Schema`, a `XId` and a walker simply named `X`. It's ensured that `XRecord` field names do not clash with the walker methods, `name_id` stores the `StringId` and `name()` is the walker method. This allows the walker to `Deref` to the struct to avoid most use of `.as_ref()` while keeping it clear what is retrieved and favoring the use of walkers through shorter names. Previously the struct field would have just been named `name`. To help with discoverability, `XId`, `XRecord`, the walker `X`, and the implementation of the `Walk` trait are always in the same file. The walker is also a dedicated struct rather than a type alias.


### Walk trait

Instead of the `schema.walk(id)` pattern, I've inversed the relationship to be `id.walk(schema)` with this trait:
```rust
pub trait Walk<G> {
    type Walker<'a>
    where
        Self: 'a,
        G: 'a;

    fn walk<'a>(self, graph: &'a G) -> Self::Walker<'a>
    where
        Self: 'a;
}
```
Now using "go to definition" with `walk` will lead in the right direction instead of a generic function. The `Walk` trait has another big advantage with multiple implementors being able to point to the same walker struct. This is particularly useful for input values as the walker was `SchemaWalker<'a, &'a InputValue>` as only some values could be accessed directly by an id.

To alleviate some of the pain experienced when manipulating `Vec<id>`, `Option<id> and `IdRange<Id>` I've added blanket implementations for those to allow writing the following:

```rust
ids.walk(schema)
   .map(|(input_value_definition, value)| (input_value_definition.name(), value)),
```

rather than

```rust
self.schema[*ids].iter().map(|(input_value_definition_id, value)| {
    let value = self.walk(value);
    (self.walk(*input_value_definition_id).name(), value)
})
```

I've hesitated a bit, but it makes writing walker-related code a lot easier when it can't be generated and it's more readable IMHO.


### Misc

- fixed missing use of coercion for default input values. While it shouldn't have led to any bugs today, everything expected it to be coerced (fixes GB-7453).
- I left a `schema.walk` method because it was just too much work to clean up `engine-v2` for this PR
- Removed the embedded `SchemaWalker` inside the various operation walkers and replaced with `&'a Schema`. Since walkers aren't defined through generics anymore, had no choice but to rework it (in the right direction).

Eventually, I'll do the same for the operation walkers but the underlying structs are more complex, so well for another time...
